WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.385 --> 00:00:07.840
Speaker 1: The real mental health issue might&nbsp;
actually be the Republican Party. The there is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:14.560
a new piece in The New Republic by Michael&nbsp;
Tomasky called The main mental health issue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:21.040
in this country is in the Republican Party. And&nbsp;
this piece makes a number of really good points.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:26.320
One of the points that the piece makes, and I&nbsp;
encourage you to read it, is that red states&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:31.600
allocate way less money for treating mental&nbsp;
illness than do blue states. And in fact, if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:37.280
look at a map and we're putting such a map up on&nbsp;
the screen, you can see that generally speaking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:42.560
red states are much lighter colored, meaning&nbsp;
they devote a much smaller share of their budgets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:49.040
towards mental health treatment that we know.&nbsp;
And so at a baseline level, this is a bad start.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:55.840
When you look back at the last many years,&nbsp;
there has really only been one major expansion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:01.040
of mental health spending and coverage,&nbsp;
and it's called the Affordable Care Act,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:05.440
also known as Obamacare. Remember, Obamacare&nbsp;
and the Affordable Care Act are the same thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:12.160
I know it's wild that I have to mention&nbsp;
that, but I do. And Republicans regularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:18.640
vote against all other attempts, as I've talked&nbsp;
about with Greg Abbott or we will talk about with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:23.360
Greg Abbott. As I talked about with Congressman&nbsp;
Tony Gonzales, Republicans regularly vote against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:28.720
attempts to increase funding and increase access&nbsp;
to mental health treatment. So at a core level,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:36.720
when we say mental health, mental illness, red&nbsp;
states are disproportionately under-funded when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:42.240
it comes to access. So it's a perfectly logical&nbsp;
conclusion. You've got more Republicans than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:46.480
Democrats in red states. Red states have&nbsp;
lower funding for mental health treatment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:53.200
Increasingly, mental illness, you would expect,&nbsp;
would skew towards being a Republican problem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.160 --> 00:02:01.200
To be clear, it doesn't mean at all that there&nbsp;
are millions of lefties and Democrats, etc.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:05.920
who struggle with mental illness. But when&nbsp;
you look at access and treatment and funding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:11.280
it's going to skew as a more as a bigger problem&nbsp;
to the right. But it's more than that. The problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:19.360
in terms of mental health isn't just&nbsp;
health care. It's also about economic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:28.480
care. We know about the crushing effect on one's&nbsp;
mood and cognitive state and mental health status&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:35.360
of being unable to take time off from work. When&nbsp;
you need it of knowing that you're going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:42.000
able to just pay your basic bills, of feeling&nbsp;
like there's no economic opportunity where you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:46.880
are. And when you look at all of these different&nbsp;
things, when you look at right wing economic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:53.520
policy versus left wing economic policy, the&nbsp;
economic realities of red states we know are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.520 --> 00:03:00.560
much more negatively impactful on mental health&nbsp;
and and sort of the state of one's mood than those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:06.960
economic policies in blue leaning states. Again,&nbsp;
these are averages we're talking generally. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:12.480
there's another layer which Tomasky doesn't&nbsp;
talk about in the piece, which I want to add,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:20.320
which is when we talk about a lot of these right&nbsp;
wing ideas, they sound almost what we might call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:26.400
mentally ill. Now, I want to be really careful.&nbsp;
We may not literally mean mentally ill in all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:32.800
of these cases. We're not stigmatizing mental&nbsp;
illness. We're also not using it as an insult.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:39.840
But when you hear the idea. Give teachers&nbsp;
guns and have them defend classrooms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:48.080
It sounds like what you would say is an insane&nbsp;
idea. It's disconnected from reality. Only someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:54.160
who has something wrong, either significant&nbsp;
ignorance or some kind of mental disturbance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:59.520
would suggest, because it doesn't make any sense.&nbsp;
And we can go further. There was a recent poll.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:05.920
20% or more of the Republican Party believes&nbsp;
at least some. Q and on conspiracies. Okay.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:13.040
Is it literally diagnostic mental illness? No,&nbsp;
it's not. But a lot of this ideology borders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:18.640
on people who are having some kind of block&nbsp;
where they're not connected to the real world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:25.680
There's a delusional nature to it. After the 2020&nbsp;
election. 57% of Trump voters believe that Trump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:31.680
won. Trump didn't win. Joe Biden won. Joe Biden&nbsp;
easily won the popular vote. Joe Biden easily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:36.880
won the electoral vote. No one state would have&nbsp;
changed that. Of the states that were in play,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:42.000
Trump didn't win. Trump lost. And yet 57% of his&nbsp;
voters believed that he had won. At some point we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:45.520
have to say, well, there's a delusional nature&nbsp;
to it. Is it diagnostically delusional? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:49.440
that you'd have to examine everybody and mental&nbsp;
health professionals would have to make the call.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:55.760
But when we talk about mental health, the fact&nbsp;
that they're saying, well, just fix guns by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.760 --> 00:04:59.360
dealing with you, fix shootings by dealing&nbsp;
with mental health rather than guns. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:03.360
at the micro level, they vote against you doing&nbsp;
that all the time. They don't want to fund mental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:10.000
health treatment. They don't want to do any of&nbsp;
it. But when we say it's a mental illness problem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:15.280
what are we really saying? And when you step back&nbsp;
and look at the state of American politics and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:20.640
look at where access to mental health treatment is&nbsp;
funded and you look at the delusional nature of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:25.920
lot of these ideas, I again, I know I always use&nbsp;
this example. We're not talking about a difference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:34.000
as to whether the top tax rate should be 20%, 35%&nbsp;
or 50%. This is a different thing. We're talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:39.600
about something that is fundamentally delusional.&nbsp;
And you need only look at the NRA convention. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:43.280
need only look at the so-called suggestions&nbsp;
that they're coming up with to deal with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:48.480
the gun violence issue, to see that. So I'm not&nbsp;
diagnosing anybody. I'm not stigmatizing mental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:52.880
illness and I'm not ridiculing mental illness. I'm&nbsp;
also not using the term mental illness as a cudgel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:59.280
to attack people with. But I am pointing out&nbsp;
that when we talk about mental illness broadly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:05.520
we really have to dig in to what is happening&nbsp;
state by state to understand the implications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:08.480
of, number one, not properly funding&nbsp;
dealing with mental health issues.&nbsp;&nbsp;

